# Shorts — manual navigation (presenter-first)

Shorts are recorded **live on YouTube**. The presenter must control pacing — not a fast auto-play slideshow.

## Default rule

**Prefer manual navigation over automatic step advance** in every Short component.

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Start with auto-play **off** (`isPlaying` default `false`) | Auto-advance steps on a timer unless the user explicitly asks |
| Advance only on **Prev / Next** (and dot jumps) | Rapid internal phase timers the presenter cannot pause |
| Show footer hint: `Step X / N — tap Next` | Hide that the viewer must press to continue |
| Pause auto-play before multi-phase walkthrough steps | Let a step race through 8+ phases on its own |

Optional **Play** may exist for preview/rehearsal, but **shipping default is manual**.

## Animation must keep running for the whole time a step is on screen

Shorts are motion-first — prefer an animated/moving visual over a static one on
every step. Manual navigation (no auto-*advance*) does **not** mean the motion
inside a step is allowed to play once on mount and then sit still. A one-shot
`initial` → `animate` entrance that settles and stops reads as broken/frozen to
a viewer who lingers on that step (which they will — this is presenter-paced,
not a timed slideshow).

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Entrance settles into a **looping idle animation** (`repeat: Infinity`, gentle pulse/breathe/cycle) that keeps running for as long as the step is visible | Entrance plays once (`scale: [0, 1.1, 1]`, no `repeat`) and the element goes fully static afterward |
| Typing/reveal effects **retype on a loop** (e.g. cycle a `key` on an interval to replay the entrance) if the step has no other continuous motion | A typed block appears once and then just sits there for the rest of the time the step is on screen |
| Multi-node or multi-line visuals cycle which item is "active" (`setInterval` + `useReducedMotion`-gated) | Every node/line settles to the same static opacity forever |

`useReducedMotion` still gates all of this to a single static frame — looping
animation is for the default (non-reduced) case only.

This is a per-step requirement, not a whole-Short requirement — check every
step's Visual sub-component individually before shipping, since it's common
for only the first step or two to have been built with a loop and the rest to
have been copy-pasted with one-shot entrances.

## Teaching examples — prompt → answer, token by token

When a Short shows **how an LLM generates text**, use a **real chat shape**:

1. **User prompt** — a plain question (e.g. `What's the capital of Egypt?`)
2. **Model answer** — built **one token at a time** after the question (e.g. `The` → ` capital` → … → ` Cairo` → `.`)

**Do not** use prefix-completion-only examples (e.g. `The capital of France is ___`) as the main story — viewers confuse that with “autocomplete the sentence” instead of “answer the question token by token.”

Each loop round must show:

- **Input to AI** — question + answer tokens generated so far (label both parts)
- **Output shape** — illustrative probability bars for the **next** token
- **Pick one** — the chosen token highlighted
- **New input** — the picked token appended to the answer; that string feeds the next round

Mark illustrative probabilities in copy (`Illustrative probabilities`).

## Internal multi-phase steps

If one slide needs several beats (input → scores → pick → concat → repeat):

- Drive phases with **Next / Prev**, not `setTimeout` chains
- Reset phase index when leaving the step
- Progress bar reflects phase index, not a fake timer

## STRICT: content must fit the frame — both axes, tabs over stacks or side-by-side

**This is a hard requirement for every Short, not a style preference.** The 9:16
phone frame has a **fixed width (~390px) and fixed height**, and the animation
zone is `overflow-hidden` — content that doesn't fit is **silently clipped**, not
scrolled or wrapped into view. This is a shipped defect every time, not a rare
edge case, and it happens on **both axes**:

- **Vertically**: one step's cards (request card + divider + result card, or 2+
  stacked panels) add up to more height than the frame has room for.
- **Horizontally**: two comparison panels placed side by side (`flex` row with
  `flex-1` children) each need more width than half the frame once they carry a
  header + badge + a line of body text — flex items don't shrink below their
  content's intrinsic width by default, so the row silently overflows and the
  frame's `overflow-hidden` clips the second panel. This is exactly as fatal as
  the vertical case and is easy to miss because it looks fine with short
  placeholder text and only breaks once real copy goes in.

**Rule: each visible card/panel stays low-content — a short label, a couple of
data rows, one line of explanation. If a step needs to show more than one card's
worth of content — whether that content would naturally stack top-to-bottom or
sit side by side — split it across tabs (or Prev/Next phases) so only one card
renders at a time.** Never grow a single card to fit more, and never place two
non-trivial content panels in the same row to "save a tab"; add a tab instead.

**Do not stack multiple content cards vertically, and do not place two content
panels side by side, inside one step.** Instead, add a small tab toggle (two
buttons, one active at a time) or reuse Prev/Next as a phase toggle so only
**one card is visible at a time**:

| Situation | Do | Don't |
|---|---|---|
| Step shows "model asks" + "tool runs" + "result" | Tab between **Request** / **Result** cards | Stack all three cards top-to-bottom in one view |
| Step compares 2+ examples, tools, or sides (tool cases, sequences, code files, "Tool A vs Tool B") | Tabs to swap the example, one full-width panel on screen | Render every example as its own stacked block, or place both side by side in a `flex` row |
| A single card's text/rows keep growing to fit more facts | Cut it down to one idea, move the rest behind a tab | Add more lines/rows until the card is tall |

Before shipping any step, mentally add up its card heights (title + padding + rows)
against the phone frame height, **and** check whether any row places 2+ non-trivial
panels side by side — if either is close, split into tabs. Don't shrink text or
padding below the legibility floor to force a fit, and don't let a card grow tall
or a row grow wide instead of adding a tab.

**Reject on sight:** a step with 2+ full content cards stacked vertically; a step
with 2+ full content cards placed side by side in a flex row; a card whose
row/line count keeps expanding instead of moving content behind a tab; any visual
not verified against the phone frame's width **and** height before shipping.

